│  Final Output: ## Executive Summary                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  This literature review investigates how multi-agent systems coordinate and  │
│  communicate, focusing specifically on architectures, conversational         │
│  protocols, and control mechanisms in Large Language Model (LLM) based       │
│  systems. The main findings indicate that modern frameworks rely heavily on  │
│  pre-configured conversable roles, multi-turn natural language dialogue,     │
│  and structured multi-agent debate to resolve conflicts and improve factual  │
│  reasoning. Furthermore, task execution is predominantly managed through     │
│  centralized orchestrators that delegate duties to specialized worker and    │
│  safeguard agents. A key caveat of this review is that it draws on a fixed   │
│  corpus of 15 papers, which restricts the analytical scope primarily to      │
│  conversational and architectural paradigms while leaving empirical          │
│  performance comparisons between centralized and decentralized topologies    │
│  largely unaddressed in the retrieved evidence.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Introduction                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Understanding how multi-agent systems coordinate and communicate is         │
│  critical for scaling artificial intelligence beyond isolated                │
│  prompt-response loops toward collaborative problem-solving. In complex      │
│  domains ranging from software development to simulated social               │
│  environments, individual agents must divide labor, exchange contextual      │
│  information, negotiate solutions, and handle errors collectively. As        │
│  language models become more autonomous, investigating their structural      │
│  organization sheds light on how collective intelligence emerges from        │
│  discrete computational instances. This review examines the foundational     │
│  architectures, communication topologies, and operational mechanics          │
│  governing multi-agent coordination. Specifically, it restates the core      │
│  research question: How do multi-agent systems coordinate and communicate?   │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Methodology                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  This review draws on a fixed corpus of 15 papers selected via semantic      │
│  search over passages to capture literature on multi-agent architectures,    │
│  communication protocols, and coordination dynamics. Themes were derived by  │
│  synthesizing recurring concepts across the search results, including        │
│  conversable agent definitions, debate mechanisms, centralized               │
│  orchestration, and classical theoretical foundations. The scope of this     │
│  review is strictly bounded by this fixed corpus of 15 papers.               │
│  Consequently, the analysis does not incorporate external literature         │
│  outside this predefined set, creating inherent limits regarding             │
│  exhaustiveness and leaving certain advanced empirical benchmarks or niche   │
│  topological variations unexamined.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Findings                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Conversational and Role-Based Agent Foundations                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Multi-agent systems utilize specialized conversable agent classes and       │
│  systematic role-playing protocols to structure collaborative networks and   │
│  drive message transmission. Foundational frameworks establish baseline      │
│  agent subclasses, such as designated assistant agents backed by LLMs and    │
│  user proxy agents that mediate interactions, which together form the        │
│  building blocks of multi-agent network topologies ((autogen_2023)).         │
│  Supporting this structural view, architectural surveys emphasize that       │
│  modern multi-agent paradigms explicitly incorporate infrastructure to       │
│  support message transmission and knowledge sharing across distinct agent    │
│  instances ((xi_2023_survey)). To operationalize these exchanges, automated  │
│  role-playing and task-specification techniques dynamically assign           │
│  functional roles—such as pairing a Python programmer assistant with an      │
│  automated user—thereby framing inter-agent communication around             │
│  well-defined occupational duties ((camel_2023)).                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Memory-Driven and Conversational Conflict Resolution                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Agents resolve conflicts and improve factual reasoning through multi-turn   │
│  conversational interactions, multi-agent debate using multiple LLM          │
│  inference instances, and long-term memory synthesis. Rather than relying    │
│  on rigid programmatic rules, agents address factual discrepancies by        │
│  constructing multiple LLM inference instances that engage in structured     │
│  debate to check reasoning and factuality ((autogen_2023)). In simulated     │
│  social environments, this conversational mechanism is reinforced by         │
│  internal cognitive architectures; agents share opinions and context         │
│  directly through natural language dialogue informed by long-term memory     │
│  streams that store, synthesize, and retrieve relevant experiences           │
│  ((generative_agents_2023)). While this conversational approach provides     │
│  flexibility, it contrasts with classical multi-agent models by              │
│  substituting deterministic negotiation protocols with probabilistic,        │
│  dialogue-based consensus building.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Orchestrated Task Execution and Centralized Safety Controls             │
│                                                                              │
│  Complex workflows are managed through centralized coordinator frameworks    │
│  that divide labor among specialized assistant and safeguard agents while    │
│  incorporating execution and safety guardrails. In orchestrated              │
│  architectures, a central commander coordinates specialized workers—such as  │
│  a writer generating code and interpretation alongside a safeguard agent     │
│  designed to prevent information leaks and malicious code execution—to       │
│  navigate complex problem spaces and execute iterative issue resolution      │
│  ((autogen_2023)). This centralized routing ensures that operational tasks   │
│  are decomposed and monitored. However, it introduces a structural           │
│  bottleneck: the entire multi-agent workflow depends heavily on the          │
│  fidelity of the central orchestrator's routing decisions and the token      │
│  overhead required to maintain conversational state across multiple          │
│  specialized worker nodes.                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ### Classical Assumptions of Veracity and Decentralized Network Behavior    │
│                                                                              │
│  Early theoretical foundations of multi-agent systems rely heavily on        │
│  assumptions of agent benevolence and veracity, alongside emergent           │
│  behaviors over electronic communication networks. Classical multi-agent     │
│  theory investigates decentralized artificial intelligence and how global    │
│  patterns emerge organically from local agent interactions without a         │
│  central director ((wooldridge_1995)). Furthermore, classical communication  │
│  models establish normative behavioral assumptions, including agent          │
│  *veracity* (the assumption that an agent will not knowingly communicate     │
│  false information) and *benevolence* ((wooldridge_1995)). These classical   │
│  formulations provide a stark contrast to modern LLM-based architectures,    │
│  highlighting a fundamental shift in how trust and reliability are handled   │
│  in multi-agent environments.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Discussion                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Synthesizing the corpus reveals key agreements, tensions, and trade-offs    │
│  in multi-agent system design. There is broad agreement across contemporary  │
│  frameworks that inter-agent coordination naturally manifests through        │
│  multi-turn natural language conversation and explicit message-passing       │
│  protocols ((xi_2023_survey), (autogen_2023), (generative_agents_2023)).     │
│  However, a significant tension emerges between modern centralized           │
│  orchestration and classical decentralized theories. While contemporary LLM  │
│  frameworks heavily favor structured, centralized orchestration—such as a    │
│  commander directing specialized workers ((autogen_2023))—classical          │
│  literature emphasizes decentralized AI and emergent behaviors where global  │
│  coordination arises organically without a central director                  │
│  ((wooldridge_1995)).                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  A profound trade-off lies in the evolution of behavioral assumptions.       │
│  Classical multi-agent systems operated on the foundational assumption of    │
│  agent *veracity* and *benevolence*, trusting that communicating peers       │
│  would not knowingly transmit false information ((wooldridge_1995)). In      │
│  contrast, modern LLM-based agents are probabilistic and prone to            │
│  hallucination, forcing developers to abandon intrinsic trust. Instead,      │
│  modern systems rely on architectural workarounds such as multi-agent        │
│  debate ((autogen_2023)) and dedicated "Safeguard" roles ((autogen_2023))    │
│  to actively verify outputs. This reliance on natural language conversation  │
│  creates an unstated architectural dependency: multi-agent coordination is   │
│  fundamentally bottlenecked by token generation speeds and context-window    │
│  constraints, transforming communication overhead into direct computational  │
│  and financial latency costs. Furthermore, the corpus exhibits a notable     │
│  gap regarding empirical performance comparisons; while theoretical          │
│  distinctions between centralized and decentralized networks are noted       │
│  ((autogen_2023), (wooldridge_1995)), rigorous comparative evaluations of    │
│  their respective efficiency, task success rates, and scalability are        │
│  absent from the reviewed literature.                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Conclusion                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Multi-agent systems coordinate and communicate primarily through            │
│  structured natural language dialogue, role-based task allocation, and       │
│  conversational debate mediated by either centralized orchestrators or       │
│  decentralized message-passing networks. While modern frameworks             │
│  successfully leverage specialized roles and multi-instance debate to        │
│  enhance reasoning and resolve factual conflicts, they remain constrained    │
│  by the inherent unreliability of underlying language models. Open problems  │
│  in the field include mitigating communication overhead, preventing error    │
│  propagation across conversational turns, and establishing rigorous          │
│  empirical benchmarks to compare centralized orchestration against           │
│  decentralized peer-to-peer agent networks.                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ## References                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  - (Wooldridge & Jennings, 1995) Intelligent Agents: Theory and Practice     │
│  [ID: wooldridge_1995]                                                       │
│  - (Xi et al., 2023) The Rise and Potential of Large Language Model Based    │
│  Agents: A Survey [ID: xi_2023_survey]                                       │
│  - (Li et al., 2023) CAMEL: Communicative Agents for Mind Exploration of     │
│  Large Language Model Society [ID: camel_2023]                               │
│  - (Yao et al., 2023a) ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language   │
│  Models [ID: react_2023]                                                     │
│  - (Park et al., 2023) Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human     │
│  Behavior [ID: generative_agents_2023]                                       │
│  - (Wu et al., 2023) AutoGen: Enabling Next-Gen LLM Applications via         │
│  Multi-Agent Conversation [ID: autogen_2023]                                 │
│                                                                              │
